st years of life  where essentially  we have handed over that period of life  to an elite class of activists and  unionists  essentially right well that's right my  high schools crenshaw high school if you  saw the movie boys in the hood  that's my high school only two percent  of kids are math proficient  now the inner city parents polls show  black and brown parents  want the ability to say uh i want to put  my kid into a charter school  a private school or religious school and  again the teachers union are adamantly  opposed to that  and when you look at where teachers pull  their own school-age kids greg  they're far more likely to have their  own kids in private school than  households that don't have public school  teachers the people that know the school  system the best  aren't putting their own kids in them  you know i want to talk to you about the  homeless situation because it's the most  obvious thing that you see whenever you  land in la or san francisco  these this is not the way people  envision homelessness as like oh this  family was thrown out of their house or  it was a homeless veteran  these are people that come with tents  and they live  they just live on the street a lot of  them are healthy a lot of them are  mentally  ill but they refuse to leave and they  have people on their side they have an  act again an elite activist class that  is there  protecting them how do you get what  would you do  well that's right what the what the what  the governor does and what the lawmakers  do in sacramento  is have what i call a housing first  policy and that is to build homes and  far more far more expensively  than the private sector would do without  dealing with the underlying reason why  people are on the streets  majority of them are mentally ill and  have substance abuse problems  those need to be dealt with there's all  sorts of treatment centers we have all  sorts of money for that  and then you need to have low cost  housing so to the extent th